        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "complexity of structures used by wealthy individuals\" and difficulties in obtaining information from other jurisdictions"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Legislative anti-avoidance responses often prompt new schemes, creating an arms race between authorities and scheme designers.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "arms race\" between tax authorities and those who design and implement avoidance schemes, highlighting that legislative responses often lead to new schemes"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "OBR costings for tax-avoidance measures have historically fallen short of estimates by 15\u201365%.",
        "evidence_id": "E10",
        "quote": "OBR costings for such measures have often fallen short of original estimates, by amounts varying between 15% and 65% in some categories"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "O14 asks whether the gap between the richest and the rest narrows. The relevant distributional question here is: who benefits from the offshore structures this policy would close? The evidence shows that the primary beneficiaries of these structures are large, often private-equity-backed care home operators and their offshore investors \u2014 a wealthy minority routing profits through Jersey, the Isle of Man, and similar jurisdictions to minimise UK tax. The 26 largest providers alone funnelled \u00a3117 million in related-party debt repayments, and 12% of care homes run by the four biggest operators are owned via offshore secrecy jurisdictions. Stopping this would redirect tax that is currently avoided back into public revenues, reducing the fiscal advantage enjoyed by wealthy offshore investors relative to ordinary taxpayers and care recipients. That is a genuine, if modest, narrowing of the gap \u2014 making it an 'improves' on O14.\n\nThe magnitude is constrained by two factors. First, enforcement: HMRC itself acknowledges the difficulty of tackling offshore non-compliance given structural complexity and information barriers from other jurisdictions. Second, the historical pattern of anti-avoidance measures yielding 15\u201365% less than projected. Both factors suggest the real-world redistributive gain will be smaller than the headline figures imply. The policy is also stated at a high level with no committed enforcement mechanism or quantified target, which keeps confidence low. The direction of effect on inequality is nonetheless clear: the current structures concentrate gains at the top; closing them moves in the opposite direction.",
